# DBaaS External Access Setup Guide

This guide will help you set up external access to your DBaaS UI with static IPs and custom domain.

## Quick Start

### 1. Get Your Cloudflare Credentials

**Get Zone ID:**
1. Log in to [Cloudflare Dashboard](https://dash.cloudflare.com/)
2. Select your domain: `synxdata.com`
3. Copy the **Zone ID** from the right sidebar (under "API" section)

**Create API Token:**
1. Go to [API Tokens](https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens)
2. Click **Create Token**
3. Use the **Edit zone DNS** template
4. Select Permissions:
   - Zone - Zone Settings - Read
   - Zone - DNS - Edit
5. Under **Zone Resources**, select your domain
6. Click **Continue to summary** → **Create Token**
7. Copy the token (you won't see it again!)

### 2. Set Environment Variable

```bash
export TF_VAR_cloudflare_api_token="your-cloudflare-api-token-here"
```

Or add to your shell profile (~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc):
```bash
echo 'export TF_VAR_cloudflare_api_token="your-token"' >> ~/.zshrc
source ~/.zshrc
```

### 3. Create Configuration File

```bash
# Copy example configuration
cp external-access.tfvars.example external-access.tfvars

# Edit the file
nano external-access.tfvars
```

Update these values:
```hcl
enable_alb_ingress       = true
dbaas_domain_name        = "synxdb-elastic-demo.synxdata.com"  # Your domain
enable_cloudflare_dns    = true
cloudflare_zone_id       = "abc123def456"  # Your Zone ID
cloudflare_proxy_enabled = false  # Start with HTTP-only, enable later for HTTPS
enable_ssl_redirect      = false
```

### 4. Update Main Configuration

Edit your `terraform.tfvars` to enable the dbaas-platform module:

```hcl
# Enable DBaaS platform deployment
deploy_dbaas_services = true
```

### 5. Apply Terraform

```bash
# Initialize (if providers changed)
terraform init

# Plan
terraform plan -var-file="external-access.tfvars"

# Apply
terraform apply -var-file="external-access.tfvars"
```

This will take approximately **10-15 minutes** to:
- Create IAM role and policy for AWS Load Balancer Controller
- Deploy AWS Load Balancer Controller via Helm
- Create Kubernetes Ingress resource
- Provision Application Load Balancer (ALB)
- Configure Cloudflare DNS

### 6. Get Access URLs

After deployment completes:

```bash
# View outputs
terraform output

# Get specific URLs
terraform output dbaas_ui_url_https
terraform output dbaas_console_url_https
```

### 7. Access Your DBaaS UI

Open in browser (HTTP initially, HTTPS after ACM certificate setup):
- **Operations Dashboard**: `http://synxdb-elastic-demo.synxdata.com/ops/`
- **User Console**: `http://synxdb-elastic-demo.synxdata.com/console/user/login`

## Verification Steps

### Check DNS Resolution

```bash
dig synxdb-elastic-demo.synxdata.com
```

Expected output (with Cloudflare proxy disabled):
```
synxdb-elastic-demo.synxdata.com. 300 IN CNAME k8s-dbaas-xxxxx.us-west-2.elb.amazonaws.com
k8s-dbaas-xxxxx.us-west-2.elb.amazonaws.com. 60 IN A xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx
```

### Check ALB Status

```bash
kubectl get ingress -n dbaas
```

Expected output:
```
NAME              CLASS   HOSTS                               ADDRESS
dbaas-ui-ingress  alb     synxdb-elastic-demo.synxdata.com   k8s-dbaas-xxxxx.us-west-2.elb.amazonaws.com
```

### Check AWS Load Balancer Controller

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l app.kubernetes.io/name=aws-load-balancer-controller
```

Expected output:
```
NAME                                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
aws-load-balancer-controller-xxxxxxxxxx-xxxxx   1/1     Running   0          5m
```

### Test Access

```bash
# Test HTTP access
curl -I http://synxdb-elastic-demo.synxdata.com/ops/

# Check ALB target health
aws elbv2 describe-target-health --target-group-arn <target-group-arn> --region us-west-2
```

## Troubleshooting

### Issue: DNS not resolving

**Solution:**
```bash
# Check Cloudflare DNS records
terraform output cloudflare_dns_records

# Verify in Cloudflare dashboard
# DNS → Records → Look for your domain
```

### Issue: 502 Bad Gateway

**Cause:** Ingress controller or backend service not ready

**Solution:**
```bash
# Check ingress-nginx pods
kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx

# Check DBaaS service
kubectl get pods -n dbaas

# Check service endpoints
kubectl get endpoints -n dbaas dbaas-integration
```

### Issue: 503 Service Unavailable

**Cause:** Backend service is not responding

**Solution:**
```bash
# Check DBaaS logs
kubectl logs -n dbaas -l app.kubernetes.io/name=synxdb-dbaas-integration

# Check service
kubectl describe svc -n dbaas dbaas-integration
```

### Issue: Cloudflare 525 Error

**Cause:** SSL handshake failed between Cloudflare and origin

**Solution:**
- Ensure `cloudflare_proxy_enabled = true`
- Use Cloudflare SSL mode: **Flexible** (in Cloudflare dashboard → SSL/TLS)
- Or configure TLS at ingress-nginx level with valid certificate

### Issue: Connection timeout

**Cause:** Security group or firewall blocking traffic

**Solution:**
```bash
# Check NLB security group
terraform output | grep security_group

# Verify nlb_allowed_cidrs includes your IP
```

## Security Best Practices

### 1. Restrict Access by IP

Configure AWS security groups for the ALB to restrict access:
```bash
# Add IP restrictions to ALB security group via AWS Console or CLI
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress \
  --group-id <alb-security-group-id> \
  --protocol tcp --port 80 \
  --cidr 203.0.113.0/24
```

### 2. Enable Cloudflare Firewall

Use Cloudflare firewall rules for additional protection:
- Configure in Cloudflare Dashboard → Security → WAF
- Add IP access rules
- Enable rate limiting

### 3. Monitor Access Logs

```bash
# Check ALB access logs (configure S3 bucket for ALB logs)
# View in S3 or use Athena for analysis

# AWS Load Balancer Controller logs
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l app.kubernetes.io/name=aws-load-balancer-controller --tail=100

# Cloudflare logs (requires Enterprise plan)
# Available in Cloudflare dashboard
```

## Updating Configuration

To change domain or settings:

```bash
# Edit configuration
nano external-access.tfvars

# Apply changes
terraform apply -var-file="external-access.tfvars"
```

## Disabling External Access

To remove external access while keeping EKS cluster:

```bash
# Edit external-access.tfvars
enable_alb_ingress = false

# Apply
terraform apply -var-file="external-access.tfvars"
```

This will remove:
- Application Load Balancer (ALB)
- AWS Load Balancer Controller
- Kubernetes Ingress resource
- IAM role and policy
- Cloudflare DNS records

But keeps:
- EKS cluster
- DBaaS application
- RDS database
- S3 buckets

## Cost Impact

**Additional Monthly Costs:**
- Application Load Balancer: ~$16-20/month ($0.0225/hour)
- ALB Load Balancer Capacity Units (LCUs): Variable based on usage
- Data transfer: Variable (typically $0.09/GB out)
- Cloudflare: Free tier (includes SSL + DDoS when proxy enabled)

**Estimated Total: ~$20-30/month** (excluding data transfer and LCUs)

